--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5285559cfa33d7041aa12c3287c4d2a7, type: 3}
  m_Name: 06_CORRECTION_AND_VERIFICATION
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::NotebookSection
  number: 06
  title: CORRECTION AND VERIFICATION
  unlocked: 1
  entries:
  - title: 18. Correction operation
    tag: 1
    body: "Once the syndrome points to a flipped qubit, apply an <b>X gate</b> to
      that data qubit. For example, if q1 has flipped from 0 to 1, applying X to
      q1 flips it back to 0.\n\n<color=#ff8a5c><b>\u258C LIMITATION</b>\n<indent=0.9em>If
      the syndrome is 00, apply <b>no</b> correction. An X on a healthy qubit would
      <b>create</b> an error.</indent></color>"
    bench: 1
    qiskit: "if fix is not None:\n    qc.x(data[fix])   # undo the flip"
  - title: 19. Verification
    tag: 1
    body: "<b>Verification</b> is a follow-up check that the corrected circuit meets
      the expected condition. Here, the encoded state started as |000\u27E9, so a
      successful repair means that measuring the three data qubits gives 000. The
      console reports success only if the simulation's actual result passes this
      rule.\n\nA wrong correction should fail verification, letting you re-read the
      syndrome and try again:\n\n<color=#ffe2a8><b>ERROR  SYNDROME  YOUR FIX   DATA 
      RESULT</b></color>\n q1      11      X on q1    000   Verified\n q1      11     
      X on q0    110   Fails: two bits wrong\n none    00      X on q2    001   Fails:
      you made an error\n q2      01      none       001   Fails: error remains\n\n<color=#c08a3e><b>\u258C
      RESEARCH NOTE</b>\n<indent=0.9em>Checking for 000 works here because the starting
      state is known. In a real experiment the encoded state is usually unknown,
      so verification uses other methods, such as repeating the syndrome measurement.</indent></color>"
    bench: 3
    qiskit: 'qc.measure(data, out)

      verified = out_bits == "000"   # decided
      by the simulation'
  - title: 20. Limitations of this code
    tag: 1
    body: "The three-qubit repetition code corrects a <b>single</b> bit-flip (X)
      error under the assumptions of this exercise. It has important limits:\n\n\u2022
      <b>Phase-flip errors go unnoticed.</b> A phase flip changes the sign, turning
      \u03B1|000\u27E9 + \u03B2|111\u27E9 into \u03B1|000\u27E9 \u2212 \u03B2|111\u27E9.
      The parity checks compare bit values only, so they cannot see it.\n\u2022 <b>Two
      errors can mislead the syndrome.</b> If q0 and q1 both flip, the data reads
      110, the syndrome is 01, and the \"fix\" (X on q2) produces 111. The encoded
      value has silently changed from |0_L\u27E9 to |1_L\u27E9.\n\u2022 <b>It is
      a teaching model.</b> Real quantum computers use larger codes (such as Shor's
      nine-qubit code or the surface code) that handle both bit-flip and phase-flip
      errors, repeat the checks continually, and allow for faulty measurements.\n\n<color=#ff8a5c><b>\u258C
      LIMITATION</b>\n<indent=0.9em>A successful verification shows how <b>this</b>
      simulated circuit behaves. It does not prove that every kind of quantum error
      has been eliminated.</indent></color>"
    bench: 3
    qiskit: 'qc.z(0)   # phase flip: the parity checks never see it

      qc.x(0);
      qc.x(1)   # two flips: syndrome 01 points to q2'
  guides:
  - label: Exact and noisy simulation with Qiskit Aer
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/simulate-with-qiskit-aer
  - label: Get started with Sampler
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/get-started-with-sampler
  - label: Overview of noise management techniques
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/error-mitigation-overview
